# Copy to .env and run "chmod 600 .env". Wrap a value in single quotes if it
# contains "$" (Compose does not expand inside single quotes).

# Version of the images to run: a published tag without the leading "v"
# (for example 0.1.0).
ASTOVIA_VERSION=
# Domain that points to this server, host name only (no https://, port or
# path). Caddy obtains the HTTPS certificate for it.
ASTOVIA_DOMAIN=
# Generate once with: openssl rand -base64 32  (keep it; changing it makes the
# stored integration tokens (GitHub, Slack, Chatwork, Google), AI credentials,
# Slack notification webhooks and Web Push keys unreadable)
ASTOVIA_JWT_SECRET=
# Optional: sign in with an OpenID Connect provider (Google, Cloudflare Access
# for SaaS, Keycloak, ...) instead of passwords. ASTOVIA_AUTH_MODE is password
# (the default when empty) or oidc. With oidc, ASTOVIA_OIDC_ISSUER,
# ASTOVIA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID and ASTOVIA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET are required; without
# it they must be empty.
# - Register the redirect URL https://<ASTOVIA_DOMAIN>/auth/oidc/callback at
#   the provider. Astovia asks for the scopes "openid email".
# - ASTOVIA_OIDC_ISSUER is the issuer of the provider, exactly as it appears
#   in its /.well-known/openid-configuration (for Google,
#   https://accounts.google.com).
# - Who can sign in is decided by invitations. An owner registers an email
#   address and a role on the user management screen; the first sign-in whose
#   verified email address equals an active invitation (valid for 7 days)
#   creates the user. From then on the user is recognized by the iss and sub
#   of the provider, not by the email address.
# - Create the first owner before the first sign-in:
#     docker compose run --rm app users create --email you@example.com --no-password
#   A user that exists and is not linked to an identity yet, such as this one
#   or a user created with a password, is linked at its first sign-in with
#   the same verified email address, without an invitation.
# - With Google as the issuer (https://accounts.google.com), the first
#   sign-in of an address outside @gmail.com needs the hd claim, which only
#   Google Workspace accounts have. A personal Google account made with an
#   address of your own domain cannot be linked, even if it signed in with an
#   earlier release. There is no setting to turn this off.
# - ASTOVIA_OIDC_REQUIRE_EMAIL_VERIFIED is true (the default when empty) or
#   false. false accepts ID tokens without the email_verified claim.
# - Cloudflare Access for SaaS: the issuer is
#   https://<team>.cloudflareaccess.com/cdn-cgi/access/sso/oidc/<client ID>,
#   and ASTOVIA_OIDC_REQUIRE_EMAIL_VERIFIED=false is needed because its ID
#   tokens have no email_verified claim. Use it only when every login method
#   of Access is a provider that verifies the email address.
# - Updating from 0.1.0: ASTOVIA_ALLOWED_EMAILS was removed, and Astovia does
#   not start while it is set. Existing users that are not disabled can sign
#   in after the update (a former owner comes back as an owner), including
#   the ones the list kept out. Take these steps in this order:
#     1. Apply the schema (docker compose run --rm migrate).
#     2. Disable everyone you kept out by leaving them off the list:
#          docker compose run --rm app users disable --email someone@example.com
#     3. Remove ASTOVIA_ALLOWED_EMAILS from .env.
#     4. Create the first owner if there is none yet (see above).
#     5. Invite the other people from the user management screen.
#   Where running the command is hard, disable them on the user management
#   screen before updating, while 0.1.0 still runs. If ASTOVIA_ALLOWED_EMAILS
#   was empty to keep everyone out, no error appears and the existing users
#   can sign in after the update: disable the ones to keep out.
# - When the identities change at the provider, linked users are refused
#   (not_allowed): ASTOVIA_OIDC_ISSUER changed (the issuer of Cloudflare
#   Access for SaaS contains the client ID, so it changes when the Access
#   application is created again), the client was created again at a provider
#   whose sub depends on the client ID, or an account was created again.
#   Clear the links so that the next sign-in links the users again:
#     docker compose run --rm app users unlink --all
#     docker compose run --rm app users unlink --email someone@example.com
#   After unlinking, a user is linked to whoever signs in with a verified
#   email address equal to the email address of the user. A user whose email
#   address changed at the provider cannot be linked again (Astovia cannot
#   change the email address of a user). If another person now uses the
#   former email address at the provider, that person is linked to the user:
#   disable such users before unlinking. unlink keeps the sessions, API
#   tokens and MCP connections; it does not stop anyone. Use "users disable"
#   to stop a user.
ASTOVIA_AUTH_MODE=
ASTOVIA_OIDC_ISSUER=
ASTOVIA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
ASTOVIA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
ASTOVIA_OIDC_REQUIRE_EMAIL_VERIFIED=
# Optional: the mail integration (Gmail / Google Workspace). Create a "Web
# application" OAuth client in Google Cloud whose authorized redirect URI is
# https://<ASTOVIA_DOMAIN>/integrations/google/callback. Set both or none.
ASTOVIA_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
ASTOVIA_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional: send notifications by mail as well. ASTOVIA_MAIL_PROVIDER is
# cloudflare (Cloudflare Email Service) or resend (Resend). With it empty, the
# other ASTOVIA_MAIL_* values and ASTOVIA_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID must be empty
# too. ASTOVIA_MAIL_FROM is a bare address (notify@example.com).
# ASTOVIA_MAIL_FROM_NAME is used by Resend only. ASTOVIA_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
# is required for cloudflare and must be empty for resend.
ASTOVIA_MAIL_PROVIDER=
ASTOVIA_MAIL_API_KEY=
ASTOVIA_MAIL_FROM=
ASTOVIA_MAIL_FROM_NAME=
ASTOVIA_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# Optional: true lets an OpenAI-compatible AI endpoint use http and private
# addresses (read "OpenAI互換の接続先" in https://astovia.dev/guides/ai/ first). Empty means false.
ASTOVIA_AI_ALLOW_PRIVATE_ENDPOINTS=
